# ============================================================================
# CLOUDFLARE PAGES ROUTING
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The site is a client-side-routed React app, so a deep link such as
# /collection/midnight-oil-spot-bowl has no file on disk. Without a fallback
# Pages answers 404 and the router never runs.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Pages applies the top-most matching rule, so the rule that
# must not be swallowed goes first.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. API passthrough — deliberately before the SPA fallback.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the intended deployment this rule never fires: the Worker is bound to
# `www.example.com/api/*` as a zone route, and a zone route is evaluated
# before the request can reach Pages at all. Cloudflare's own documentation
# notes that `_redirects` only applies to static asset responses, so an
# /api request handled by the Worker never consults this file.
#
# It exists for the case where that binding is missing or misconfigured.
# Without it, `/*` below would answer an API call with 200 and a page of
# HTML, and the client would report a JSON parse error — a misleading
# symptom that points at the frontend instead of at the missing route. With
# it, a missing Worker binding produces a 404 on a JSON request, which names
# the actual problem.
#
# A 200 proxy to the same path is what Pages supports for this (the docs
# table lists proxying as relative-URL-only and rejects other status codes).
/api/*  /api/:splat  200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. SPA fallback — everything else renders the app shell.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*  /index.html  200
